mpared to SAFREE and DES. While DES preserves generation quality, it fails to effectively erase the target concept in SD-v3.5. 5.2.2 White-Box Robustness To further illustrate the robustness of our method, we evaluate it under white-box adversarial scenarios. As shown in Figure 3 , our method consistently outperforms all baselines and achieves the lowest attack success rate across all three white-box attacks: UnlearnDiff, MMA, and Ring-A-Bell. The results in Figure 3 and Table 1 reveal that methods performing erasure in the text encoder are more robust against adversarial attacks while better preserving generation quality. Figure 4 : Qualitative examples on artistic style erasure. Method Erase “Van Gogh” Erase “Kelly McKernan” LPIPS e ↑ \uparrow LPIPS u ↓ \downarrow LPIPS d ↑ \uparrow LPIPS e ↑ \uparrow LPIPS u ↓ \downarrow LPIPS d ↑ \uparrow SD-v1.4 ESD 0.40 0.26 0.14 0.37 0.21 0.16 UCE 0.25 0.05 0.20 0.25 0.03 0.22 SAFREE 0.39 0.25 0.14 0.33 0.28 0.05 GLoCE 0.18 0.01 0.17 0.35 0.01 0.34 AdvUnlearn 0.40 0.22 0.18 0.37 0.28 0.09 DES 0.16 0.12 0.04 0.28 0.09 0.19 Ours ( TEA ) 0.40 0.18 0.22 0.38 0.22 0.16 SD-v3.5-large SAFREE 0.41 0.49 -0.08 0.35 0.34 0.01 DES 0.40 0.35 0.05 0.41 0.40 0.01 Ours ( TEA ) 0.46 0.26 0.20 0.39 0.30 0.09 Table 2 : Evaluation results for artist erasure. 5.3 Artistic Style Removal Beyond explicit content, we evaluate TEA on artistic style erasure for two target artists, Van Gogh and Kelly McKernan Gong et al. (2024) . We use LPIPS ( Zhang et al., 2018 ) to measure perceptual distance between images generated by the original and erased models: LPIPS e on prompts referencing the target artist, LPIPS u on prompts referencing other artists, and LPIPS d = LPIPS e − LPIPS u {}_{d}=\mathrm{LPIPS}_{e}-\mathrm{LPIPS}_{u} . As shown in Table 2 , on SD-v1.4 our method achieves the highest LPIPS e on both artists, and the highest LPIPS d for Van Gogh. Training-free methods such as UCE and GLoCE attain very low LPIPS u , but their erasure lags behind tra